--TEST--
a namespace has no declaration and no card, so a cursor on a segment in front of the type answers what the namespace holds — the classes and enums directly under it, each with its line where its row carries one, `rule:core-api/reserved-namespace`
--FILE--
<?nvs
var $verb = Core\Ht<|>tp\Method::Get;
echo $verb;
--REQUEST--
hover
--EXPECT--
```nvs
namespace Core\Http
```

- `Chunks` — The pieces of a streamed reply's body, in the order they arrived. A `foreach` over `Core\Http\Stream::chunks()` gives each piece as `tainted bytes`.
- `Client` — Sends HTTP requests to other servers, with one method for each HTTP verb. Each call returns the reply as a `Core\Http\Response`.
- `Event` — One server-sent event: its data, its name and its id. A `foreach` over `Core\Http\Stream::events()` gives one for each event the server finished sending.
- `Events` — The server-sent events of a streamed reply's body, in the order they arrived. A `foreach` over `Core\Http\Stream::events()` gives each one as a `Core\Http\Event`.
- `Identity` — A client certificate and its key. A request sends it when the server asks the client to prove who it is.
- `Lines` — The lines of a streamed reply's body, in the order they arrived. A `foreach` over `Core\Http\Stream::lines()` gives each line as a `tainted string`.
- `Method` — The closed set of HTTP verbs a `#[Route]` may be declared under and a request may carry — eight of them, safe ones first so that the four the CSRF check covers are the contiguous tail from `Post` on; `CONNECT` is deliberately absent.
- `Part` — Describes a request body, or one field of a multipart body. A file part is read from disk while it is sent, so the whole file is never in memory.
- `Response` — The reply to an outbound HTTP call: its status code, its headers, its body, and the TLS connection it came over. `Core\Http\Client::get()` returns one.
- `Socket` — A WebSocket connection your program opened to another server: it sends and receives messages until one side closes it. `Core\Http\Client::openSocket()` returns one.
- `Stream` — A reply whose body is read while it arrives. `status()` and the header readers work at any time. The body is read once, with one of `events()`, `lines()`, `chunks()` or `saveTo()`.
- `Target` — A URL that `Core\Http::allowUrl` checked, with the addresses it was approved for. A call connects only to those addresses, so a second name lookup cannot send it somewhere else.
- `TlsInfo` — The details of the TLS connection a reply came over: the TLS version, the cipher, the server's certificates, and whether they were checked. `Core\Http\Response::tls()` returns one.
